From b7bfddf9073fd60d0ebd1e10c0411a83c29be375 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Liviu Nicoara <lnicoara@thinkoid.org>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 17:45:40 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Add a native Linux AArch64 host configuration []

The Linux configurations assume an x86_64 host: the gcc configuration
asks for libquadmath, the shared Linux platform file says long double
is the x87 format, and host-defaults.cmake has no AArch64 branch. On an
AArch64 machine a native build fails on quadmath.h.

This adds the configuration for a native AArch64 Linux host with GCC,
without changes to the front end:

- support/arch/aarch64: the AAPCS64 LP64 ABI as gcc on Linux uses it.
  Plain char and wchar_t are unsigned, long double is IEEE binary128,
  glibc's jmp_buf is 39 longs. Of the 32-bit ARM variants of the
  Itanium C++ ABI that the front end selects for ARM64, gcc uses only
  the pointer-to-member-function representation; array cookies, the
  static guard, constructors and the key function follow the generic
  ABI. Zero-width and unnamed bit-fields raise struct alignment. All
  values measured against gcc 16.2.1 on an AArch64 Linux machine.
  It sits beside arch/arm64, which is Apple's ABI.
- support/platform/linux-aarch64 and support/compiler/gcc/linux-aarch64:
  import the shared Linux and gcc files and override the x86 facts
  (long double format, __float80, libquadmath; APPROXIMATE_QUADMATH as
  the clang configuration does).
- linux-aarch64-gcc and linux-aarch64-gcc-release macro configurations,
  and the matching presets. The release preset builds the runtime
  library under the default target's name, as the macOS presets do.
- host-defaults.cmake: an aarch64 branch.
- bases/cmake-native/linux-aarch64/gcc: an edg_eccp_config that reads
  the header search lists and the gcc version from the resident gcc,
  and passes -D__float128=_Float128 to the C compiler, since gcc on
  AArch64 has no __float128 and the front end's _Float128 support
  comes with it. The base ships no predefined macro table: the table
  describes one gcc, so it is generated in lib/ on the machine the
  base is used on, with util/make_predef_macro_table; the config stops
  with that instruction when the table is missing.

The existing support/target/linux_aarch64 is left as it is. Only the
x86_64 platform imports it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
